.\" herdmux(1): the full manual, embedded in the binary (herdmux man).
.TH HERDMUX 1 "" "herdmux" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
herdmux \- an AI\-agent awareness layer for tmux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY herdmux
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.YS
.SY herdmux
.B \-\-help
|
.B \-\-version
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B herdmux
runs on top of an existing
.BR tmux (1)
setup and shows what every coding agent (claude, opencode or pi) in every
tmux session is doing: working, waiting for the user, or finished.
It flags the agents that need the user and gets the user to them in a keypress.
.PP
herdmux is a single binary that never stays running.
tmux key bindings, tmux hooks and the agents' own hooks call it, and it keeps
all of its state in tmux pane options.
.PP
It is turned on by
.B herdmux init
run from
.IR tmux.conf ,
which
.B herdmux install
adds.
init applies the saved colorscheme, sets the status bar formats, binds the
keys from
.IR config.toml ,
sets
.B HERDMUX=1
in the tmux global environment, and installs its tmux hooks.
The agent integrations do nothing unless
.B HERDMUX=1
is set, so agents run outside herdmux's tmux are unaffected.
.PP
Popups (agent picker, window organiser, launch prompt, sessionizer and
colorscheme picker) are drawn in the active colorscheme.
The status bar shows one coloured state icon per agent in each window tab,
fills the tab of an agent with Attention, and shows the session name, the
active pane's directory, its git branch and a dirty marker.
.SH COMMANDS
Flags may appear anywhere among the arguments, and
.B \-\-
ends flag parsing.
Every command accepts
.BR \-\-help .
Commands marked
.I (tmux)
must run inside tmux; outside it they exit 2 with
.RB \(lq "herdmux: not inside tmux" \(rq.
.TP
.B herdmux init
.I (tmux)
Turn the layer on.
Run it from
.IR tmux.conf :
.BR "run\-shell \(aqherdmux init\(aq" .
It is idempotent.
A broken
.I config.toml
or an unknown or invalid saved colorscheme is shown as a red message and
logged; init carries on with the defaults.
.TP
.B herdmux picker \fR[\fB\-\-client\fR \fIname\fR]
.I (tmux)
Open the agent picker popup: every agent with its session, window, state and
age, and a live preview of the selected pane.
See
.B KEY BINDINGS
for its keys.
.TP
.B herdmux organiser \fR[\fB\-\-client\fR \fIname\fR]
.I (tmux)
Open the window organiser popup for the client's session: reorder, rename,
kill and jump to windows, with a live preview.
.TP
.B herdmux next\-attention \fR[\fB\-\-client\fR \fIname\fR]
.I (tmux)
Jump to the next agent with Attention, across sessions:
.B needs\-input
before
.BR done ,
oldest first.
With none, a short message is shown and the client stays where it is.
.TP
.B herdmux launch \fR[\fIagent\fR] [\fB\-\-client\fR \fIname\fR]
.I (tmux)
With an
.IR agent ,
the
.B name
of an
.B [[agent]]
entry in
.IR config.toml ,
open a new window named after it in the current pane's directory, type its
.B command
into the window's shell (which stays after the agent exits) and switch to it.
With no argument, open the launch prompt popup: the menu of agents whose
command is on
.BR PATH ;
press an agent's key to launch it, or q or Esc to close.
.TP
.B herdmux sessionizer \fR[\fB\-\-client\fR \fIname\fR]
.I (tmux)
Open the sessionizer popup: a session switcher and project launcher.
See
.BR SESSIONIZER .
.TP
.B herdmux colorscheme \fR[\fIname\fR | \fB\-\-list\fR] [\fB\-\-client\fR \fIname\fR]
With no argument
.IR (tmux) ,
open the colorscheme picker popup, which previews each scheme live on the
whole tmux UI; Enter saves the highlighted scheme, Esc or q reverts.
With a
.I name
.IR (tmux) ,
apply that scheme and save it as the choice; an unknown or invalid scheme is
refused and nothing is saved.
With
.BR \-\-list ,
which works anywhere, print every scheme: a
.B *
on the saved choice, name, family, tone (dark or light), source (bundled,
user or override), and notes
.RB ( active
inside tmux, or
.BI "invalid: " reason\fR),
then the saved choice and the file it is saved in.
.TP
.B herdmux list \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR]
.I (tmux)
List every agent in tmux order, with columns SESSION, WINDOW, AGENT, STATE,
ATTENTION and PANE, or
.B no agents
when there are none.
With
.BR \-\-json ,
print a JSON array of objects with the fields
.BR session ,
.BR window_index ,
.BR window_name ,
.BR window ,
.BR pane ,
.BR agent ,
.BR state ,
.BR attention ,
.B since
(epoch seconds of the last state change) and
.BR pid .
.TP
.B herdmux mcp
Serve the herdmux MCP server over stdio, so an agent can read and change
herdmux's setup.
It refuses to start (exit 1) unless
.B HERDMUX=1
is set.
Tools:
.BR read_config ,
.BR write_config ,
.BR list_colorschemes ,
.BR get_colorscheme ,
.BR set_colorscheme ,
.BR write_colorscheme ,
.B delete_colorscheme
and
.BR list_agents .
Config and colorscheme writes are validated before they are saved, and key
changes apply live.
.B set_colorscheme
and
.B list_agents
need to run inside tmux; the file tools work anywhere.
.TP
.B herdmux report \fIstate\fB \-\-agent \fIname\fR [\fB\-\-pid \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-pane %\fIN\fR]
.I (tmux)
Report an agent's state for a pane; this is the integration point any agent
can call.
.I state
is
.BR working ,
.BR needs\-input ,
.B done
or
.B exit
(the agent left the pane).
.B \-\-agent
is required and is
.BR claude ,
.B opencode
or
.BR pi .
.B \-\-pid
is the agent's pid (default: report's parent pid);
.B \-\-pane
is the pane id (default:
.BR $TMUX_PANE ).
It always exits 0; errors go to the log.
.TP
.B herdmux install \fR[\fB\-\-mcp\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
Append a marked
.B run\-shell \(aqherdmux init\(aq
block to
.I tmux.conf
and install the claude hooks, the opencode plugin and the pi extension (see
.BR FILES ).
Before writing anything it checks that
.B herdmux
is on
.BR sh 's
PATH (and on the running tmux server's global PATH, if there is one), that
every JSON file it edits parses, and that every target directory is writable;
if any check fails it lists every problem and writes nothing.
Each existing file is backed up to
.IB file .herdmux.bak
before its first edit.
It is idempotent, and never touches the running tmux server; reload with
.BR "tmux source\-file ~/.config/tmux/tmux.conf" .
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print a diff of every change and write nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-mcp
Also register
.B herdmux mcp
with each agent (claude and pi must be on PATH).
.RE
.TP
.B herdmux uninstall \fR[\fB\-\-purge\fR]
Remove exactly what install added.
The
.B .herdmux.bak
backups are left in place.
A running tmux server keeps the layer until it is restarted.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-purge
Also remove herdmux's config directory.
.RE
.TP
.B herdmux man
Show this manual.
When standard output is a terminal and
.BR man (1)
is on PATH, the page is shown with
.BR "man \-l \-" ;
otherwise the roff source is printed, so
.B herdmux man > herdmux.1
saves it.
.TP
.B herdmux help \fR[\fB\-\-all\fR | \fIcommand\fR]
Show the command list, or one command's usage and flags.
.B \-\-all
includes the hidden commands.
.B herdmux \-\-help
is the same as
.BR "herdmux help" .
.TP
.B herdmux \-\-version
Print the version.
.PP
The
.B \-\-client
flag names the tmux client to act for; the key bindings pass
.BR \(aq#{client_name}\(aq .
Errors from a run with
.B \-\-client
(or inside a popup) are shown as a red message on that client and logged.
.SS Hidden commands
These are called by tmux hooks and the agent integrations, and are listed by
.BR "herdmux help \-\-all" .
They always exit 0 and log errors.
.TP
.B herdmux hook claude
The claude hook adapter: one claude hook event as JSON on stdin becomes a
state report for
.BR $TMUX_PANE .
Without
.B HERDMUX=1
and
.B TMUX_PANE
it does nothing.
.TP
.B herdmux tmux\-hook \fIevent\fR [\fIargs\fR]
The entry point for herdmux's tmux hooks:
.B pane\-focus\-in
.I window_id
(a Visit),
.B git\-refresh
.I pane_id
and
.BR recheck .
.SH KEY BINDINGS
.B herdmux init
binds these keys by default:
.TP
.B prefix a
Agent picker
.RB ( picker ).
.TP
.B prefix u
Jump to the next agent with Attention
.RB ( next\-attention ).
.TP
.B prefix r
Window organiser
.RB ( organiser ).
.TP
.B prefix o
Launch an agent
.RB ( launch ).
.TP
.B prefix g
Sessionizer
.RB ( sessionizer ).
.TP
(unbound)
Colorscheme picker
.RB ( colorscheme ).
.PP
Change them in the
.B [keys]
section of
.IR config.toml ;
the action names are the ones in parentheses.
A value is
.BI "\(dqprefix " key \(dq
(a key after the tmux prefix),
.I \(dqkey\(dq
alone (a key in the root table, pressed without the prefix, such as
.BR \(dqM\-a\(dq ),
or
.B \(dq\(dq
to unbind.
Key names follow tmux: an optional
.BR C\- ,
.B M\-
or
.B S\-
modifier (or
.BR ^ )
in front of one character or a named key such as
.BR Enter ,
.B F5
or
.BR PageUp .
For example:
.PP
.EX
[keys]
sessionizer = "prefix f"
colorscheme = "prefix C"
next\-attention = "M\-u"
launch = ""
.EE
.PP
An unknown action, a bad key name or two actions on one key make all of
.B [keys]
fall back to the defaults, with the error shown in red.
A key that herdmux moves away from or unbinds gets back whatever was bound
there before (tmux's default or tmux.conf's binding).
The bindings carry notes, so they show in tmux's \fBprefix ?\fP list.
Changes apply at the next
.B herdmux init
(a tmux.conf reload), or at once through the MCP
.B write_config
tool.
.SS Keys inside the popups
In every popup, Ctrl-N and Ctrl-P move like j/k (also while typing a
filter) and Ctrl-Y does what Enter does.
.TP
Agent picker
j/k or the arrows move, Enter jumps to the agent, / filters (by agent, place
and state; Enter keeps the filter, Esc clears it), Tab switches between all
sessions and this session, s toggles sorting by place or Attention first,
n launches a new agent (then the agent's key), x stops the selected agent
(y confirms; the pane stays), q closes, and Esc clears a kept filter or closes.
.TP
Window organiser
j/k or the arrows move, gg and G go to the first and last window, Enter jumps
to the window, space grabs it (j/k then carry it, space or Enter drops it,
Esc puts it back), r renames (Enter saves, Esc cancels), x kills (y confirms;
the session's last window cannot be killed), u undoes moves and renames,
q or Esc closes.
.TP
Launch prompt
Each agent's key launches it; q or Esc closes.
.TP
Sessionizer
It opens typing into the filter: letters fuzzy-filter the list by name (the
matched letters are highlighted, best matches first), Ctrl-N/Ctrl-P or the
arrows move, Enter switches (see
.BR SESSIONIZER ),
and Esc clears the filter or, when it is empty, closes.
The right side shows the selected entry's full path and, for a session, a
live preview of its active pane, or, for a directory, the session Enter
would switch to or create and the directory's contents.
.TP
Colorscheme picker
j/k or the arrows move and preview, g/Home and G/End go to the first and last
scheme, / filters by name and family, Enter saves the highlighted scheme,
and q or Esc reverts to the saved one (Esc first clears a kept filter).
.SH CONFIGURATION
herdmux's configuration lives in its config directory:
.B $HERDMUX_CONFIG_DIR
if set, else
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/herdmux
if
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is set to an absolute path (a relative one is ignored), else
.IR ~/.config/herdmux .
It holds:
.TP
.I config.toml
The launch menu, the keys and the sessionizer roots.
.TP
.I colorscheme
The saved colorscheme's name, written by the picker,
.B herdmux colorscheme
.I name
and the MCP
.B set_colorscheme
tool.
Missing or empty means
.BR flexoki\-dark .
.TP
.IR colors/ name .toml
The user's own colorschemes, or overrides of bundled ones (see
.BR COLORSCHEMES ).
.SS config.toml
Every section is optional; a missing file means all defaults.
The file is read when it is needed (by
.B herdmux init
for keys, and by the popups and
.B herdmux launch
for the menu and roots), so menu and root changes apply at the next popup.
.TP
.B [[agent]]
The launch menu, in order.
Any valid entry replaces the whole default menu, which is:
.RS
.PP
.EX
[[agent]]
name = "claude"
key = "c"
command = "claude"
\&
[[agent]]
name = "opencode"
key = "o"
command = "opencode"
\&
[[agent]]
name = "pi"
key = "p"
command = "pi"
.EE
.TP
.B name
Required.
The new window's name and the argument to
.BR "herdmux launch" .
Up to 32 letters, digits,
.BR . ,
.B _
or
.BR \- ,
starting with a letter or digit.
.TP
.B command
Required.
One line, typed into the new window's shell as is.
The entry is shown only if the command's first word (after any leading
.IB VAR = value
words) is an executable on
.BR PATH ,
so one config works on any machine.
.TP
.B key
Optional.
One printable, non\-space character, not
.B q
(which closes the prompt).
When omitted, the first letter or digit of the name not already taken is
used.
.PP
An entry with a missing or bad name, command or key makes the menu fall back
to the defaults.
A later entry with a duplicate name or key is dropped.
.RE
.TP
.B [keys]
One entry per action:
.BR picker ,
.BR next\-attention ,
.BR organiser ,
.BR launch ,
.B sessionizer
and
.BR colorscheme .
Defaults:
.RS
.PP
.EX
[keys]
picker = "prefix a"
next\-attention = "prefix u"
organiser = "prefix r"
launch = "prefix o"
sessionizer = "prefix g"
colorscheme = ""
.EE
.PP
See
.B KEY BINDINGS
for the value format.
.RE
.TP
.B [sessionizer]
.RS
.TP
.B roots
A list of directories whose subdirectories the sessionizer offers, each an
absolute path
.RB ( ~
is not expanded).
Default: none (only sessions are listed).
.TP
.B depth
How many levels below each root to list.
Default: 3.
.PP
See
.BR SESSIONIZER .
.RE
.PP
A broken file never leaves herdmux without a config.
Bad TOML or a value of the wrong type means all defaults; a broken
.B [[agent]]
entry means the default agents; a bad
.B [keys]
entry means the default keys; a
.B [sessionizer]
root that is not an absolute path is left out (the other roots are kept);
an unknown key elsewhere is only reported.
Problems are shown in red, starting
.RB \(lq config.toml: \(rq,
by
.BR "herdmux init" ,
the launch prompt, the picker and the sessionizer.
.SH SESSIONIZER
The sessionizer
.RB ( "herdmux sessionizer" ,
.B prefix g
by default) switches between tmux sessions and opens project directories as
sessions.
To bind it elsewhere set, for example,
.B sessionizer = \(dqprefix f\(dq
in
.BR [keys] ;
.B \(dq\(dq
unbinds it.
.PP
Its list always starts with every open tmux session, sorted by name, each
with a count of its agents.
Then come the directories found under the
.B [sessionizer]
roots, sorted by name.
With no roots it lists only sessions.
.PP
.EX
[sessionizer]
roots = ["/home/user/projects", "/home/user/src"]
depth = 2
.EE
.PP
.B roots
are absolute paths.
They are used exactly as written:
.B ~
and environment variables are not expanded.
A root that is not absolute (such as
.I projects
or
.IR ~/projects )
is left out and reported in red in the sessionizer, for example
.RS
.PP
.EX
config.toml: [sessionizer] roots: "projects" is not an absolute path (~ is not expanded)
.EE
.RE
.PP
The other roots are still listed.
.PP
.B depth
counts levels below each root, and every level is listed, not only the
deepest: the root itself, each directory in between, and the directories
.B depth
levels down.
.B depth = 0
lists only the roots themselves.
A missing or negative
.B depth
silently means 3.
Hidden directories (names starting with
.BR . )
and everything inside them are skipped, and directories that cannot be read
are skipped silently.
Large trees with the default depth can produce long lists; a lower depth or
narrower roots keep them short.
.PP
Enter on a session switches the client to it.
Enter on a directory switches to the session named after the directory,
creating that session (starting in the directory) first if it does not
exist.
The name is the directory's base name with everything before its first ASCII
letter dropped (typically a numeric prefix of digits, dots and dashes), then
each \fB.\fP and \fB:\fP turned into \fB_\fP, as tmux does for session names.
For example,
.I 25.14\-herdmux
becomes
.B herdmux
and
.I 12.35\-my.proj
becomes
.BR my_proj .
Two directories that give the same name share one session.
.SH COLORSCHEMES
A colorscheme styles the whole tmux look: pane backgrounds, status bar,
window tabs, borders, popups, menus and messages, as well as everything
herdmux draws.
One is chosen by name.
.PP
25 schemes are bundled:
catppuccin\-frappe, catppuccin\-latte, catppuccin\-macchiato,
catppuccin\-mocha, flexoki\-dark, flexoki\-light, gruvbox\-dark,
gruvbox\-light, kanagawa\-dragon, kanagawa\-lotus, kanagawa\-wave,
onedark\-cool, onedark\-dark, onedark\-darker, onedark\-deep,
onedark\-light, onedark\-warm, onedark\-warmer, rose\-pine\-dawn,
rose\-pine\-main, rose\-pine\-moon, tokyonight\-day, tokyonight\-moon,
tokyonight\-night and tokyonight\-storm.
The default is
.BR flexoki\-dark .
.PP
A user scheme is a file
.IR colors/ name .toml
in the config directory.
The name must start with a letter or digit and contain only letters, digits,
.B _
and
.B \-
(at most 64 characters); other files there are ignored.
A user file with a bundled scheme's name replaces that scheme entirely.
The file needs a
.B [colors]
table with all 16 slots as
.B \(dq#rrggbb\(dq
strings:
.PP
.EX
[colors]
pane_bg               = "#100f0f"
bg                    = "#1c1b1a"
bg_raised             = "#403e3c"
bg_overlay            = "#282726"
fg                    = "#cecdc3"
fg_muted              = "#878580"
fg_on_attention       = "#100f0f"
border                = "#403e3c"
border_active         = "#ce5d97"
accent                = "#ce5d97"
state_working         = "#d0a215"
state_needs_input     = "#d14d41"
state_done            = "#879a39"
state_unknown         = "#878580"
attention_needs_input = "#d14d41"
attention_done        = "#879a39"
.EE
.PP
Other keys and tables are ignored.
An invalid scheme is listed with the reason and cannot be chosen.
If the saved choice is unknown or invalid,
.B herdmux init
falls back to flexoki\-dark (the user's override of it, if valid) and shows
a warning; the saved file is not changed.
.SH AGENT STATES AND ATTENTION
An
.I agent
is a tmux pane in which an agent CLI (claude, opencode or pi) is running.
It belongs to the session its pane is in; moving the pane moves the agent.
claude reports through its hooks, opencode through a plugin and pi through an
extension, all calling herdmux; any program can report with
.BR "herdmux report" .
.PP
Each agent has one
.IR "agent state" :
.TP
.B working
The agent is working.
.TP
.B needs\-input
The agent is blocked on the user: a permission prompt or a question.
.TP
.B done
The agent finished its turn; it is the user's move.
.TP
.B unknown
An agent process recognised by its name only, with no state reporting (for
example, an agent started without its integration or before
.BR "herdmux init" ).
.PP
An agent that exits or dies, or is suspended or put in the background, is
dropped.
.PP
.I Attention
is a sticky flag on an agent, raised whenever it moves into
.B needs\-input
or
.B done
while its window is not shown in any attached client.
Its kind follows the agent's latest
.BR needs\-input / done
state.
When Attention is raised, herdmux rings the terminal bell once and shows a
message naming the sessions.
Attention never times out.
It is cleared only by a
.IR Visit :
the agent's window being shown in an attached client, which clears
Attention on every agent in that window, whatever their state (the agent's
pane does not need to be active).
An agent that exits or dies takes its Attention with it.
.PP
In the status bar each window tab shows one state icon per agent; a tab whose
agent has Attention is filled with the
.B attention_needs_input
or
.B attention_done
colour.
.B prefix u
.RB ( next\-attention )
jumps to the agent with Attention that is most urgent:
.B needs\-input
before
.BR done ,
oldest first.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HERDMUX_CONFIG_DIR
The config directory, instead of
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/herdmux
or
.IR ~/.config/herdmux .
.TP
.B HERDMUX
Set to
.B 1
in the tmux global environment by
.BR "herdmux init" .
The agent integrations and
.B herdmux mcp
do nothing without it.
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
If set to an absolute path, the log is
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/herdmux/log .
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
If set to an absolute path, the config directory is
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/herdmux
(unless
.B HERDMUX_CONFIG_DIR
is set), and
.B herdmux install
and
.B uninstall
use
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tmux/tmux.conf
and
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/opencode/ .
.TP
.BR TMUX ", " TMUX_PANE
Set by tmux; they tell herdmux which server and pane it runs for.
.TP
.B PATH
.B herdmux
must be on
.BR sh 's
PATH, because tmux runs key bindings and hooks through
.BR sh .
The launch menu shows only agents whose command is on PATH.
.PP
.B HERDMUX_POPUP
and
.B HERDMUX_CLIENT
are set by herdmux for its own popups and are not meant to be set by hand.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/herdmux/config.toml
The configuration (or
.I config.toml
in
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/herdmux
or
.BR $HERDMUX_CONFIG_DIR ;
see
.BR CONFIGURATION ).
.TP
.I ~/.config/herdmux/colorscheme
The saved colorscheme's name.
.TP
.IR ~/.config/herdmux/colors/ name .toml
User colorschemes and overrides.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/herdmux/log
The log (or
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/herdmux/log ).
.TP
.I ~/.config/tmux/tmux.conf
install adds the block between
.B # >>> herdmux >>>
and
.BR "# <<< herdmux <<<" .
.TP
.I ~/.claude/settings.json
install adds claude hook entries running
.BR "herdmux hook claude" .
.TP
.I ~/.config/opencode/plugins/herdmux\-agent\-state.js
The opencode plugin.
.TP
.I ~/.pi/agent/extensions/herdmux\-agent\-state.ts
The pi extension.
.TP
.IB file .herdmux.bak
The original of each file install edited, saved before its first edit.
.PP
With
.BR "install \-\-mcp" ,
herdmux is also registered as an MCP server with claude
.RB ( "claude mcp add \-s user herdmux \-\- herdmux mcp" ,
plus read\-tool permissions in
.IR ~/.claude/settings.json ),
opencode (in
.IR ~/.config/opencode/opencode.json )
and pi
.RI ( ~/.pi/agent/mcp.json ).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on failure, and 2 for usage errors and
.RB \(lq "not inside tmux" \(rq.
.BR report ,
.B hook
and
.B tmux\-hook
always exit 0.
.SH EXAMPLES
Install, check what would change first, then reload tmux:
.PP
.EX
herdmux install \-\-dry\-run
herdmux install
tmux source\-file ~/.config/tmux/tmux.conf
.EE
.PP
Report a state from a script running in an agent's pane:
.PP
.EX
herdmux report needs\-input \-\-agent claude \-\-pid "$AGENT_PID"
.EE
.PP
Choose a colorscheme from the shell:
.PP
.EX
herdmux colorscheme \-\-list
herdmux colorscheme tokyonight\-night
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tmux (1)
